<p class="copy">
	Co-H2O/E2 forms the second half of the <?php echo sf_lore_link('fuel-gates', 'fuel gate'); ?>/water gate duopoly of technologies that underpin space travel and colonization in the settled systems. For nearly all purposes, Slim Water is identical to its mundane counterpart. Indeed, its paraphysical effect wasn’t even discovered until the water gate had been commercialized. Liquid Co-H2O/E2 demonstrates hypercompressibility between the temperatures of 12°C and 94°C. As the liquid is compressed, porous ice-like crystal structures form throughout the material, allowing the remaining unfrozen H2O molecules to wedge into the ice’s crystal lattice, compressing it down to 8.35% of its original volume.
</p>
<p class="copy mb-0">
	While the chemical’s compressibility has been less important than the simple fact that it’s clean water available through a <?php echo sf_lore_link('switch-gates', 'switch gate'); ?>, its paraphysical effect has still allowed for ship designs without the bulky water tanks that were once required. Given that the material will rapidly expand to its original volume when outside of its temperature range, most ships are built with overpressure valves venting the material either into space or into waterproofed overflow zones. More than a few deck crews have startled awake to find their quarters flooded. With a paraphysical decay half-life of two years, the material will eventually return to its original volume through simple transmutation back into mundane water. Given the impossibility of separating slim and fat water, “water cycling” has become standard in all ship maintenance and repair routines.
</p>
